 ## Overview
-Analytic Gradient Computation computes the 3D gradient vector for local shading (BRDF) in volume rendering by evaluating the exact first-order partial derivatives of the basis reconstruction function [S1]. It sets itself apart from [Gradient Computation via Finite Differences](1077-gradient-computation-via-finite-differences.md) by requiring no additional memory accesses or ray traversals, relying solely on the data values already loaded for the scalar evaluation [S1]. Unlike [Gradient Map Generation](1204-gradient-map-generation.md) which precomputes gradients, this method computes them on-the-fly during ray marching [S1].
+Analytic Gradient Computation computes the 3D gradient vector for local shading (BRDF) by deriving exact derivatives of the basis reconstruction function, avoiding the additional memory accesses and ray traversals required by finite difference methods [S1]. It is distinguished from its parent method, [Gradient Computation via Finite Differences](1077-gradient-computation-via-finite-differences.md), by calculating gradients from existing data values rather than sampling offset positions, and from [Analytic Gradient Computation via Box-Spline Derivatives](3199-analytic-gradient-computation-via-box-spline-derivatives.md) by its general applicability to standard basis reconstruction without relying on specific FCC lattice symmetries [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-- Sample point $p = (p_x, p_y, p_z)$ in world space [G].
-- Cell width $C_w$ [S1].
-- Cell center coordinates $C_p = (C_{px}, C_{py}, C_{pz})$ for each contributing cell [G].
-- Basis function component $\hat{h}(\cdot)$ [S1].
-- Scalar data values $C_v$ and basis weights $\hat{H}_C(p)$ for all contributing cells $C$ [S1].
-
-**Outputs:**
-- Gradient vector $\nabla B(p) = (\frac{\partial B(p)}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial B(p)}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial B(p)}{\partial z})$ [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+- Input: The scalar volume data values $C_v$, the basis reconstruction function $\hat{H}_C(p)$, the cell width $C_w$, and the sample position $p$.
+- Output: The gradient vector $\nabla B(p) = [\frac{\partial B}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial B}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial B}{\partial z}]$.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- The method operates on the scalar reconstruction result $B(p)$ and its components. No new storage is required beyond registers for the gradient components [S1].
+- $C_v$: Scalar value associated with cell $C$.
+- $C_{py}$, $C_{pz}$: Coordinates of the cell center relative to the sample point in the y and z dimensions.
+- $C_w$: Width of the cell.
+- $\hat{h}(\cdot)$: The 1D basis function component.
 
-**Steps:**
-1. Compute the scalar reconstruction $B(p)$ and the weighted sums of basis derivatives $\sum_C \frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial x} C_v$, $\sum_C \frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial y} C_v$, and $\sum_C \frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial z} C_v$ for each axis using the data values already loaded for the scalar evaluation [S1].
-2. Compute the partial derivative of the basis function $\frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial x}$ for each cell $C$ using the analytic formula below [S1].
-3. Compute the partial derivative of the reconstructed scalar field $B(p)$ using the quotient rule applied to the basis reconstruction equation [S1]:
+**Formulas:**
+The gradient of the reconstructed signal $B(p)$ is computed using the quotient rule on the basis reconstruction sum [S1]:
+
 $$
 \frac{\partial B(p)}{\partial x} = \frac{\sum_C \hat{H}_C(p) \sum_C \frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial x} C_v - \sum_C \hat{H}_C(p) C_v \sum_C \frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial x}}{(\sum_C \hat{H}_C(p))^2}
 $$
-4. Repeat Step 3 for $y$ and $z$ axes to obtain the full gradient vector [S1].
 
-**Formulas:**
 The partial derivative of the basis function component $\hat{H}_C(p)$ with respect to $x$ is:
+
 $$
 \frac{\partial \hat{H}(p)}{\partial x} = \hat{h}\left(\frac{|C_{py} - p_y|}{C_w}\right) \hat{h}\left(\frac{|C_{pz} - p_z|}{C_w}\right) \chi(x) \frac{1}{C_w}
 $$
+
 The sign function $\chi(t)$ is defined as:
+
 $$
-\chi(t) = \begin{cases} -1, & \text{if } C_{pt} - p_t \geq 0 \\ 1, & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
+\chi(t) = \begin{cases} -1, & \text{if } C_{p_t} - p_t \geq 0 \\ 1, & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
 $$
 
+**Steps:**
+1. For the current sample position $p$, identify all contributing cells $C$ within the basis support.
+2. For each cell $C$, compute the basis weight $\hat{H}_C(p)$ and the data value $C_v$.
+3. Compute the partial derivative of the basis function $\frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial x}$ using the formula above, which reuses the basis function evaluations from the y and z dimensions and applies the sign function $\chi(x)$ scaled by $1/C_w$.
+4. Accumulate the sums required for the quotient rule:
+   - $S_H = \sum_C \hat{H}_C(p)$
+   - $S_{HV} = \sum_C \hat{H}_C(p) C_v$
+   - $S_{dH} = \sum_C \frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial x}$
+   - $S_{dHV} = \sum_C \frac{\partial \hat{H}_C(p)}{\partial x} C_v$
+5. Compute the gradient component $\frac{\partial B(p)}{\partial x}$ using the quotient rule formula.
+6. Repeat steps 3-5 for $y$ and $z$ dimensions to obtain the full gradient vector.
+
 **Parameters:**
-- $C_w$: Cell width. Not given in the sources.
+- $C_w$: Cell width, determined by the spatial indexing and level structure [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- The analytic gradient is not continuously differentiable, leading to shading artifacts at cell and level boundaries, whereas central differences are continuous but expensive [S1].
-- The sign function $\chi(t)$ introduces discontinuities in the derivative at the cell boundaries ($C_{pt} - p_t = 0$) [S1].
